Verwenden Sie `SET LOCAL` innerhalb einer Transaktion:

```sql
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL ivfflat.probes = 10;
SELECT ...
COMMIT;
```

[chunk 41]

Die Dokumentation empfiehlt, `hnsw.iterative_scan` (bzw. `ivfflat.iterative_scan`) auf `relaxed_order` zu setzen, da dies bessere Recall-Werte liefert, und dann eine **materialized CTE** zu verwenden, um trotzdem eine strikte Reihenfolge zu erhalten:

```sql
SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;
# or
SET ivfflat.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;
```

```sql
WITH relaxed_results AS MATERIALIZED (
    SELECT id, embedding <-> '[1,2,3]' AS distance FROM items WHERE category_id = 123 ORDER BY distance LIMIT 5
) SELECT * FROM relaxed_results ORDER BY distance + 0;
```

Hinweis: `+ 0` ist für Postgres 17+ nötig [chunk 49][chunk 50].

Zusätzlich wird empfohlen, bei Abfragen mit Distanzfilter eine materialized CTE zu verwenden und den Distanzfilter außerhalb davon zu platzieren, für beste Performance [chunk 50].

According to the documentation, the planner doesn't consider out-of-line (TOAST) storage in cost estimates, which can make a serial scan look cheaper than a parallel scan:

"The planner doesn't consider [out-of-line storage](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/storage-toast.html) in cost estimates, which can make a serial scan look cheaper." [chunk 80][chunk 81]

To address this, you can reduce the cost of a parallel scan for a query with:

```sql
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL min_parallel_table_scan_size = 1;
SET LOCAL parallel_setup_cost = 1;
SELECT ...
COMMIT;
```

or choose to store vectors inline:

```sql
ALTER TABLE items ALTER COLUMN embedding SET STORAGE PLAIN;
```
